 it's  clear  family policy generosity is greater in  sweden norway and finland and they have  lower levels of child poverty and the  way that might work is allowing two  parents to go out to work  and subsidize child care  preschool education we know is important  and  looking right across europe of the  countries where children are not getting  access to pre-school education it's a  soluble problem and it's not being sold  i think i may have got this from clyde  actually looking at  enrollment in preschool  in cuba and other latin american  countries  and reading levels in sixth grade  so it's looking at the percent of  children getting at the high level of  reading in sixth grade and cuba which  has the highest enrollment in preschool  has the best reading scores in latin  america  and the lower the enrollment  in preschool education the lower the  reading scores  in these different latin american  countries cuba's not the richest of the  latin american countries but it's got  the highest enrollment in preschool and  the best reading schools at that age  and we can see a difference in piece of  scores the program of international  student assessment by  whether or not children had a year or  more of preschool education so at age 15  there's better scores on maths literacy  and science if children were enrolled in  preschool  than if they weren't  after accounting for socio-economic  background  so profound effects and i showed you the  graph on educational mortality and i  said  this is a manifestation of this life  course effect of early child development  what happens  preschool what happens in schools  i have to show you this it's sort of  departing slightly because i get asked  commonly  other examples where people have reduced  the gradient  where they've made a difference  this is stunting by family income in  brazil  for different years of surveys so  1974 five at the top and 2006 7  stunting by family income  they have  closed the gap they've made the gradient  shallower they've really done it  it's possible i could show you under  five mortality in bangladesh  they've reduced the social gradient in  under five mortality in bangladesh  so it's not the case that inequalities  are always getting bigger  they are in some cases but we've got  important and inspiring examples  where the social gradient can get  shallower  miso level  sweden is interesting  i was giving a lecture in sweden  last year  and  a parliamentarian said  you know most reports get forgotten your  who commission report is still being  discussed in the swedish parliament five  years later  and you have to come to the parliament  so i went to the swedish parliament  and i said to them with respect  if you were not just discussing it but  doing something about it  and then i told them  i had the previous day addressed the  swedish association of local authorities  and regions 300 people representing  local authorities from all over sweden  who were all  developing plans to implement the who  commission on social determinants of  health in malmo they read the who  commission they said our national  government's not doing anything about it  we'll do it in the city of malmo and it  was set up by the deputy mayor not by  the health people  they asked me when they finished imam i  was sort of the  padronne of the malmo commission they  said what should we do now i said get on  the road and talk about it tell  everybody in sweden what you've done  and they have been  and their six areas for action were very  similar to our who and my english review  you saw this in neil slide uh slides  just an example of the social gradient  when somebody from mcmaster said this  morning what about  a living  wage and what i wanted to say was look  if you look by  quintiles of income  it's a gradient  yes we've got to deal with poverty yes  we've got to deal with the living wage  but we want everybody to have the level  of early child development  that's available to the best of  hence the whole idea and thank you  michelle for before the break for  talking about proportionate universalism  that's why we talked about  proportionate universalism we want  universalist policies we want everybody  to get up to the top level  but we may have to work harder the more  deprived  and again clyde was a great influence on  me the importance of monitoring  after we published the english review  on the one year anniversary in 2011 on  the two year anniversary and we'll do it  again this year on the four-year  anniversary we published a set of  indicators the marmot indicators and one  of which was the percent of children age  five achieving a good level of  development  by  level of affluence and deprivation of  local authorities and what you can see  is the more affluent the local authority  the better the level of early child  development  i presented this in the british  parliament some joker said i think the  proportion of variance explained would  be quite low in that correlation i  thought that's what mps most want to  discuss the multiple r squared  i said what the gentleman was trying to  say is this variance around the line  which suggests two strategies one is  reduce poverty and i've shown you  examples of how fiscal policy can make  that better or worse  but the second is to ask the question  why for a given level of affluence and  deprivation some are doing better than  others  and i was invited to birmingham the  english city of birmingham  they said they had six  marmot champions one for each of my six  domains of recommendations and they had  one on early childhood  and what they were doing  we heard a lot before about learning  from experience what they did was they  took off the shelf programs that had  been evaluated elsewhere  have proven effectiveness and they  applied them and what happened  birmingham is more deprived than the  english average so if you look in 2007  the level of early child development in  birmingham as predicted from the level  of deprivation was worse than the  english average  and look what happened in three years  look what happened in three years  they closed the gap  in three years  wow  it is rocket science  they took some evaluated programs and  they applied them  and they worked  it can make a difference  that's pretty good  why am i showing you this  my new friends are the firefighters  i went to  merseyside liverpool this is west  midlands where birmingham is  because west midlands wrote and said  they wanted to become partners with us  the fire and rescue service i went to  liverpool  and the firemen  adopted me captured me or something they  took me to a fire station  and they said  we're all macho white men  and we'd go off and fight a fire and  somebody would have died and we'd come  back and we'd say  ah what a tragedy  they really shouldn't have happened  somebody should have done something  about preventing that fire  and then they said  somebody should have done something  maybe we should have done something we  spent six percent of our time fighting  fires  yeah they play poker whatever they do  the rest of the time that why shouldn't  we do it so they went into people's  houses and talked about fitting smoke  alarms and while they were there people  would say smoke alarms i've got a leaky  roof  and these burly macho white men became  social workers they say you know you can  get a grant from the local authority to  fix your roof  we can help you do that  and then they said do you know who dies  in fires you know mentally ill  heavy smokers alcoholics  so we better get people not to smoke in  bed and then they said while we're up  why don't we try and get people not to  smoke  and then they gave older people  cards so they could get into the gym in  the fire station so older people could  come and exercise then they got the kids  and they teamed up with liverpool  football club  and they got the kids off the street  and got them playing football 